\section*{FPRAS for perfect matchings in general graphs}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 113; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that perfect matchings in arbitrary simple undirected graphs admit an FPRAS with polynomial work on every tape and certain zero output when no matching exists.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For every explicitly encoded finite general graph $G$, let $Z(G)$ count its perfect matchings. Given rational $0<\varepsilon<1$ and $0<\delta<1/2$, the claimed uniform classical randomized algorithm returns $\widehat Z\in\mathbb Q_{\ge0}$ with
\[
 \Pr[(1-\varepsilon)Z(G)\le\widehat Z\le(1+\varepsilon)Z(G)]\ge1-\delta.
\]
It outputs zero surely if $Z(G)=0$, and its worst-case bit cost is polynomial in the complete input length, $\varepsilon^{-1}$ and $\log\delta^{-1}$; bipartiteness is not required.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 113; 2026-09-23), weighted matching laws are enlarged to expose local partition ratios as marked edges. Subdivision controls unmatched-vertex costs, and alternating overlays plus a two-coordinate energy inequality bound the chain variance needed for annealing and counting.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 113; 2026-09-23).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 113; 2026-09-23), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: perfect matchings in arbitrary simple undirected graphs admit an FPRAS with polynomial work on every tape and certain zero output when no matching exists.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{BinaryMatching.lean}, \texttt{MatchingEntropy.lean}, \texttt{MatchingEntropyBounds.lean}, \texttt{MatchingFPRAS.lean}, \texttt{SingletonLoopMatching.lean}, \texttt{TriangleFace.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.BinaryMatching.deterministic\_approximate\_counting}, \texttt{OAI.LoopMatching.fullEndpoint}, \texttt{OAI.MatchingEntropy.entropy\_main}, \texttt{OAI.MatchingEntropyBounds.Refined.pointwise\_entropy}, \texttt{OAI.MatchingFPRAS.thm\_main}, \texttt{OAI.TriangleFace198.triangle\_expansion\_face}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

